export const DATA_37: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-044", num: 44, title: "Đề 044", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Value of a Close Friend", paragraphs: ["Most people can name several acquaintances — colleagues they greet in the hallway, neighbours they wave to, or classmates they sit beside. But when asked to think of someone they can truly rely on, someone they would call in a moment of crisis or share their deepest worries with, the list becomes much shorter. A close friend is different from a casual acquaintance in ways that go far beyond the amount of time two people have spent together. Researchers who study human relationships describe close friendship as one of the most important sources of wellbeing in a person's life — and one that is increasingly difficult to maintain in modern times.", "What makes a friendship \"close\"? Psychologists point to several key qualities. The first is trust — the confidence that a friend will keep your secrets, support you honestly, and not take advantage of your vulnerabilities. The second is emotional availability: a close friend is someone who listens attentively, responds with empathy, and makes you feel genuinely understood. The third quality is consistency — the sense that the friendship does not depend entirely on convenience or shared circumstance, but will endure even when life changes. Studies show that people who feel they have at least one close friend who meets these criteria report significantly higher levels of happiness, lower rates of anxiety, and a greater sense of meaning in their daily lives.", "The health benefits of close friendship extend well beyond emotional comfort. A landmark study published in the journal PLOS Medicine, which followed over three hundred thousand participants across multiple countries, found that people with strong social connections — including close friendships — had a fifty percent higher chance of survival over a given period compared to those who were socially isolated. The researchers noted that the effect of social isolation on mortality was comparable to smoking fifteen cigarettes a day, and greater than the risks associated with obesity or physical inactivity. These findings have led many public health researchers to argue that loneliness should be treated as a serious health concern, not merely a personal or emotional difficulty.", "Despite its importance, close friendship has become harder to maintain for many adults. Work demands, family responsibilities, geographical mobility, and the rise of digital communication have all contributed to a thinning of social bonds. A survey conducted in the United States found that the number of people who reported having no close friends at all tripled between 1985 and 2021. Among men in particular, social isolation has become increasingly common: research suggests that many adult men rely almost exclusively on their romantic partner for emotional support, leaving them highly vulnerable when that relationship ends or becomes strained. Cultural norms that discourage emotional openness among men may play a significant role in this pattern.", "Making and maintaining close friendships in adulthood requires deliberate effort in ways that childhood friendships rarely do. As children, proximity and shared routine — attending the same school, living in the same neighbourhood — naturally create the conditions for friendship to develop. As adults, those conditions must often be created intentionally. Researchers who study adult friendship recommend investing regularly in one-on-one time, moving conversations beyond surface topics, and being willing to show vulnerability — sharing personal struggles or fears rather than presenting only a polished version of oneself. They note that many friendships stall at a pleasant but shallow level simply because both parties are waiting for the other to go first.", "Technology has complicated the picture further. Social media platforms allow people to maintain a sense of connection with a large number of acquaintances simultaneously, which can create the illusion of a rich social life without providing the depth that characterises true friendship. Scrolling through a friend's photos or liking their posts requires little effort and offers limited emotional reciprocity. Several studies have found that heavy social media use is associated with increased feelings of loneliness rather than reduced ones, particularly when it substitutes for face-to-face interaction. This does not mean that technology cannot support genuine friendship — video calls, for example, can maintain closeness across distance — but it suggests that the medium matters considerably.", "Ultimately, the value of a close friend lies not in the number of pleasant moments shared but in the sense of being truly known and accepted by another person. In a world that increasingly rewards performance, productivity, and self-sufficiency, the quiet reciprocity of a deep friendship offers something that cannot easily be replaced: the experience of mattering to someone for exactly who you are, without pretence or condition."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["How adults can use social media more effectively to build and maintain meaningful friendships", "Scientific evidence proving that social isolation is more dangerous to health than smoking or obesity", "Why people in modern societies have more acquaintances but fewer genuine friends than previous generations", "The importance of close friendship, what makes it valuable, the challenges of maintaining it, and how technology affects it"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is identified as one of the key qualities of a close friendship?", options: ["Living close to each other so that meeting up is convenient and effortless", "The confidence that a friend will be honest, keep secrets, and not exploit your weaknesses", "Spending a large amount of time together every week without exception", "Sharing the same hobbies, interests, and social background"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the PLOS Medicine study find about people with strong social connections?", options: ["They had a fifty percent greater chance of survival over a given period compared to socially isolated people", "They reported three times more daily happiness than those with only casual acquaintances", "They were fifty percent less likely to develop anxiety or depression over their lifetime", "They were significantly less likely to smoke or engage in physically harmful behaviours"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the United States survey reveal about close friendship trends?", options: ["Geographic mobility is the single biggest factor in the decline of close adult friendships", "The number of people with no close friends at all tripled between 1985 and 2021", "The average American adult has fewer than two genuine close friends regardless of age or gender", "More than half of American adults now rely entirely on digital platforms for social connection"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do researchers recommend for adults who want to build close friendships?", options: ["Recreating the childhood conditions of shared schools and neighbourhoods as closely as possible", "Joining online communities that match people with others who share the same professional interests", "Relying on romantic partners to fulfil the need for emotional depth and close connection", "Investing in regular one-on-one time, going beyond surface topics, and being willing to show personal vulnerability"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["romantic partners who provide emotional support", "cultural norms that discourage emotional openness", "many adult men who rely almost solely on their partner for emotional support", "researchers who study social isolation among men"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a finding or claim about social media and friendship?", options: ["Social media can create the illusion of a rich social life without providing true emotional depth", "Video calls can help maintain closeness between friends who live far apart", "Heavy social media use has been linked to greater feelings of loneliness rather than reduced ones", "Platforms that limit daily usage time have been shown to improve the quality of real-world friendships"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"trust\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a social obligation to return favours and support given by others", "the belief that someone is reliable, honest, and will not misuse your confidence in them", "the feeling of comfort that comes from spending time regularly with familiar people", "a formal agreement between two parties to behave honestly in a professional context"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"vulnerability\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the risk of being taken advantage of in a professional or financial relationship", "a tendency to become overly dependent on others during periods of difficulty", "physical weakness caused by illness or a lack of proper nutrition", "the openness of sharing one's genuine fears, struggles, or imperfections with another person"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 5 about why many adult friendships remain shallow?", options: ["Adults are naturally less emotionally open than children and find it harder to form genuine bonds as they age", "Many friendships do not deepen because both people wait for the other to take the first step toward greater openness", "Adults lack the time and energy needed to invest in deep friendships because of work and family demands", "Most adult friendships stall because the two people involved no longer share enough common interests"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Evolution of Foreign Travel", paragraphs: ["The desire to journey beyond one's homeland is as old as human civilisation itself. Merchants crossed deserts and sailed unknown seas in pursuit of trade; pilgrims endured months of hardship to reach sacred sites; diplomats ventured into foreign courts to forge alliances. Yet the idea of travelling abroad for leisure — simply for the pleasure of experiencing different places, cultures, and ways of life — is a surprisingly recent phenomenon. For much of recorded history, long-distance travel was the exclusive preserve of the wealthy, the powerful, or the professionally obligated. The transformation of foreign travel into a mass activity available to ordinary people represents one of the most significant social changes of the past two centuries.", "The earliest form of organised leisure travel is often traced to the Grand Tour, a tradition that emerged among the European aristocracy and educated elite during the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. Young men of means — primarily from Britain, but also from France, Germany, and the Netherlands — were sent on extended journeys through France, Switzerland, and above all Italy, with the stated aim of completing their cultural and intellectual education. Rome, Florence, and Venice were considered essential stops, offering exposure to classical antiquity, Renaissance art, and the remnants of empires that formed the cultural bedrock of Western civilisation. The Grand Tour typically lasted between several months and several years, depending on the wealth and ambition of the traveller, and produced a genre of travel writing that shaped European intellectual life for generations.", "The arrival of the railway in the nineteenth century democratised travel in ways that the Grand Tour's aristocratic patrons could not have imagined. For the first time, large numbers of middle-class families could afford to travel significant distances within their own countries and, eventually, across borders. Thomas Cook, a British entrepreneur, recognised this emerging appetite and in 1841 organised what is widely regarded as the first modern package tour — a railway excursion for temperance campaigners in England. Cook went on to develop an entire industry around organised group travel, offering guided tours to Switzerland, Egypt, and eventually around the world. His innovations — fixed itineraries, pre-booked accommodation, group pricing — established the template for the mass tourism industry that would follow.", "The most dramatic expansion of foreign travel, however, came in the second half of the twentieth century, driven by the proliferation of commercial aviation. The introduction of jet aircraft in the late 1950s dramatically reduced the time and cost of long-distance travel, and the deregulation of the airline industry in the United States in 1978 — followed by similar moves in Europe and elsewhere — intensified competition among carriers, pushing fares lower still. By the 1990s, the budget airline model pioneered by carriers such as Southwest in the United States and Ryanair in Europe had made international air travel financially accessible to a broad segment of the population in wealthy countries. The annual number of international tourist arrivals worldwide grew from approximately 25 million in 1950 to over 1.4 billion in 2019, immediately before the COVID-19 pandemic temporarily reversed this trend.", "Alongside these structural changes, the motivations and expectations of travellers have evolved considerably. Early leisure travellers of the nineteenth century sought cultural enrichment, social distinction, and the thrill of encountering the genuinely unfamiliar. Today's travellers pursue a far wider range of experiences: adventure tourism, wellness retreats, culinary exploration, volunteer programmes, and \"slow travel\" — the deliberate choice to spend extended time in a single destination rather than rushing through multiple stops. The rise of digital platforms such as Airbnb, TripAdvisor, and Instagram has also transformed how travellers plan, document, and share their experiences, creating feedback loops that amplify certain destinations and experiences while leaving others unexplored.", "The environmental cost of this expansion has attracted growing attention. Aviation accounts for approximately two to three percent of global carbon dioxide emissions annually — a figure that, while seemingly modest, is compounded by the additional warming effects of contrails and other high-altitude emissions. The concentration of visitors in popular destinations has also produced what researchers call \"overtourism\" — a condition in which the volume of tourists degrades the very qualities that made a destination appealing in the first place. Cities such as Venice, Barcelona, and Dubrovnik have experienced significant tension between local residents and the tourism industry over issues of noise, pricing, and the displacement of communities from historic neighbourhoods.", "The future of foreign travel will be shaped by competing pressures: the continued desire of a growing global middle class to explore the world, the urgent need to reduce the carbon footprint of aviation and tourism infrastructure, and the challenge of managing visitor flows in ways that preserve the cultural and ecological integrity of destinations. Whether the industry can reconcile these tensions through technological innovation, behavioural change, and smarter regulation — or whether the growth of the past century will prove environmentally unsustainable — remains one of the defining questions facing the travel sector in the decades ahead."], questions: [{ q: "What does the passage mainly discuss?", options: ["The role of technology companies such as Airbnb and TripAdvisor in transforming modern travel behaviour", "The environmental damage caused by the rapid growth of the global tourism industry since the 1950s", "How foreign travel evolved from an activity of the privileged few into a global mass phenomenon, and the challenges this has created", "Why the Grand Tour was a more meaningful form of travel than the package holidays popular today"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the primary stated purpose of the Grand Tour?", options: ["To complete the cultural and intellectual education of young men from privileged backgrounds", "To allow wealthy families to escape the harsh winters of northern Europe", "To expose young travellers to foreign languages in preparation for careers in diplomacy", "To give young aristocrats the opportunity to establish business connections across Europe"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was significant about Thomas Cook's railway excursion in 1841?", options: ["It demonstrated that steam-powered travel could reduce journey times by more than half", "It was the first journey to cross an international border using rail transport", "It introduced the concept of solo independent travel to a middle-class audience for the first time", "It is widely considered the first modern package tour and established the template for mass tourism"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did the number of international tourist arrivals change between 1950 and 2019?", options: ["It rose from 25 million to 250 million before the COVID-19 pandemic reversed all growth", "It grew from approximately 25 million to over 1.4 billion", "It doubled every decade, growing from 50 million to just under one billion", "It increased tenfold from 140 million to over 1.4 billion"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following problems is associated with \"overtourism\"?", options: ["The rapid depletion of natural resources in coastal areas caused by the construction of hotels and resorts", "The inability of airlines to handle rising passenger volumes, leading to widespread delays and cancellations", "The economic decline of rural areas as tourism investment concentrates exclusively in major cities", "A condition in which excessive tourist numbers degrade the very qualities that made a destination attractive"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a type of travel experience pursued by today's travellers?", options: ["Educational exchange programmes at foreign universities", "Adventure tourism and wellness retreats", "Slow travel involving extended stays in a single destination", "Culinary exploration and volunteer programmes"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["culinary and adventure tourism operators offering niche experiences", "digital platforms such as Airbnb, TripAdvisor, and Instagram", "travellers who use digital tools to plan and share journeys", "popular destinations that are amplified by online feedback loops"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"democratised\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["improved in quality so that more people chose to participate", "made subject to government regulation and public oversight", "transformed into a competitive market with many rival providers", "made available and affordable to a much wider range of people"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"proliferation\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a sharp and widespread increase in the number or amount of something", "the replacement of an older system by a more efficient modern alternative", "the official introduction of a new technology by a government body", "the gradual improvement in safety standards across an industry"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view of the future of foreign travel based on the final paragraph?", options: ["The author implies that governments should immediately introduce strict limits on international tourist arrivals to protect fragile destinations", "The author is confident that technological innovation will resolve the environmental challenges of aviation within the next decade", "The author believes that the growth of global tourism will inevitably slow due to the environmental damage it causes", "The author sees the future of travel as uncertain and dependent on how well competing economic, environmental, and social pressures are managed"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Artificial Intelligence Research in Higher Education", paragraphs: ["Universities have long served as the primary incubators of scientific discovery, and the field of artificial intelligence is no exception. From the foundational work of Alan Turing at Cambridge and John McCarthy at Stanford and MIT, to the deep learning breakthroughs that emerged from academic laboratories in Montreal, Toronto, and New York, the intellectual architecture of modern AI was built largely within university walls. Yet the relationship between academia and AI is undergoing a profound and perhaps irreversible transformation. As commercial investment in AI has surged to levels that dwarf public research budgets, many observers have begun to ask whether universities can still play a meaningful role in shaping the trajectory of one of the most consequential technologies in human history.", "The financial gap between industry and academia has become stark . Training a single state-of-the-art large language model can now cost tens or even hundreds of millions of dollars in computing resources alone — a figure that places frontier AI research beyond the reach of all but the most generously funded universities. In response, many institutions have pursued strategic partnerships with technology companies, accepting grants, donated computing credits, and access to proprietary datasets in exchange for research collaboration and, in some cases, preferential access to graduating talent. These arrangements have enabled some universities to remain at the cutting edge of applied AI research, but they have also raised persistent questions about the independence of academic inquiry and the extent to which corporate priorities quietly shape research agendas.", "The talent pipeline presents a parallel challenge. Senior AI researchers capable of leading large-scale projects command salaries in industry that academic institutions simply cannot match. A full professor of computer science at a leading research university might earn between 150,000 and 250,000 US dollars annually — a compensation package that appears generous by most academic standards but is dwarfed by the multi-million-dollar offers routinely extended by major technology companies. [1] The result has been a sustained exodus of senior researchers from universities to industry, leaving many departments struggling to attract and retain the expertise needed to supervise doctoral students, secure competitive grants, and maintain credible research programmes. Some departments have responded by redefining their role: rather than competing with industry on frontier model development, they focus on areas where academic values add distinct value — interpretability, fairness, long-term safety, and the broader social implications of AI.", "Despite these pressures, universities retain several advantages that industry cannot easily replicate. Academic researchers are, at least in principle, free to pursue questions that are intellectually important but commercially unattractive — to study failure modes, to challenge prevailing assumptions, and to publish findings that may be inconvenient for companies with products in the market. The peer review process, however imperfect, provides a mechanism for independent scrutiny that internal corporate research cannot fully substitute. Universities also serve as training grounds for the next generation of AI researchers: even if many graduates ultimately move into industry, the foundational skills, critical habits of mind, and ethical frameworks they develop in academic settings shape how they approach their work throughout their careers. [2]", "The question of who controls access to data and computing infrastructure has emerged as perhaps the most consequential structural tension in AI research today. Large technology companies possess proprietary datasets of a scale and richness that no university can assemble independently, and they control the cloud computing platforms that now underpin virtually all large-scale AI training. When companies choose to withhold data, restrict access to models, or publish only selective research findings, the academic community's ability to independently evaluate, replicate, and critique industry claims is significantly curtailed. Several prominent researchers have argued that this asymmetry of access constitutes a form of epistemic control — a capacity to shape what the broader scientific community knows and believes about the capabilities and limitations of cutting-edge AI systems.", "Governments in several regions have begun to respond to these structural imbalances. The European Union's investment in AI research infrastructure through programmes such as Horizon Europe, and the establishment of national AI research institutes in countries including the United Kingdom, Canada, and France, represent attempts to provide universities with computing resources and collaborative frameworks which they hope will reduce dependence on corporate infrastructure. In the United States, the National AI Research Resource — a federally funded initiative designed to give academic researchers access to high-performance computing — has moved from proposal to pilot phase, though critics argue that the scale of funding remains insufficient to meaningfully offset industry's structural advantages. [3]", "The stakes of this contest extend well beyond the academic labour market or the allocation of research funding. The questions that AI researchers choose to ask, the datasets they use to train and evaluate systems, and the benchmarks by which progress is measured all carry significant normative weight — they embed assumptions about what intelligence is, what it is for, and whose interests it should serve. If those questions are asked predominantly by researchers operating within commercial imperatives, the answers are likely to reflect those imperatives. [4] Universities, at their best, represent a countervailing force: institutions with the mandate, the tradition, and at least some of the independence needed to ask uncomfortable questions on behalf of the public. Whether they can preserve that capacity in the face of the current wave of commercialisation is a question with consequences that reach far beyond the academy."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage primarily concerned with?", options: ["The ethical obligations of technology companies toward the academic institutions whose research formed the basis of their products", "The growing tensions between academic AI research and commercial interests, and whether universities can maintain a meaningful independent role", "The historical contributions of universities to the development of artificial intelligence since the mid-twentieth century", "Why government funding for university AI research has consistently failed to match the scale of private sector investment"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how have many universities responded to the financial gap between academic and industry AI research?", options: ["By concentrating all available funding on a single flagship research project rather than spreading resources across many teams", "By replacing costly computational research with purely theoretical work that requires fewer resources", "By forming strategic partnerships with technology companies in exchange for grants, computing credits, and data access", "By lobbying governments to impose limits on commercial AI spending in order to level the playing field"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what salary range might a full professor of computer science at a leading research university typically earn?", options: ["Between 250,000 and 500,000 US dollars annually", "Over one million US dollars annually, comparable to mid-level industry roles", "Between 80,000 and 120,000 US dollars annually", "Between 150,000 and 250,000 US dollars annually"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how have some university departments responded to their inability to compete with industry on frontier model development?", options: ["They have focused on areas such as interpretability, fairness, safety, and the social implications of AI where academic values add distinct value", "They have introduced mandatory industry placement years so that doctoral students gain experience in commercial settings", "They have merged with other departments to pool computing resources and reduce overall research costs", "They have entirely suspended AI research until adequate government funding is secured"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the current status of the National AI Research Resource in the United States?", options: ["It is still in the proposal stage and has not yet received formal government approval", "It has moved from a proposal to a pilot phase, though critics argue the funding remains insufficient", "It was rejected by Congress and replaced with a series of smaller state-level research grants", "It has been fully funded and is now operational at all major research universities nationwide"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT identified as an advantage that universities retain over industry in AI research?", options: ["Their role as training grounds for the next generation of AI researchers", "The freedom to pursue questions that are intellectually important but commercially unattractive", "The peer review process as a mechanism for independent scrutiny of research findings", "Greater access to large proprietary datasets that are not available to commercial organisations"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"stark\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["growing steadily over time and likely to continue", "sharply obvious and impossible to overlook or minimise", "surprising given the long history of academic leadership in this field", "unfair and in need of urgent policy intervention"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"consequential\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["arising as a direct result of earlier decisions made by governments", "relatively minor in practice despite attracting significant public attention", "having important and far-reaching effects or implications", "difficult to resolve because it involves conflicting legal obligations"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 6 (\"collaborative frameworks which they hope will reduce dependence on corporate infrastructure\") refers to _______.", options: ["governments in several regions that have begun responding to structural imbalances", "technology companies that control cloud computing platforms", "universities that benefit from government investment programmes", "critics who argue that current funding scales remain insufficient"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? \"This dynamic, some argue, risks producing a generation of researchers who are technically brilliant but institutionally conditioned to ask only the questions that industry deems worth answering.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Resilience of Traditional Music in a Globalized Era", paragraphs: ["When the first wave of mass-produced popular music swept across the globe in the mid-twentieth century, carrying with it the rhythms and instrumentation of Western rock, jazz, and pop, many ethnomusicologists feared that the world's extraordinary diversity of traditional musical forms would be gradually eroded beyond recovery. The concern was not unfounded: in the decades that followed, commercially dominant genres did exert powerful homogenising pressures on local musical cultures in virtually every region of the world. Yet the story of traditional music in the twenty-first century has proved considerably more complicated — and in many respects more hopeful — than those early predictions suggested. Traditional musical forms have demonstrated a capacity for adaptation, reinvention, and renewed relevance that challenges any simple narrative of cultural decline.", "The mechanisms through which traditional music has sustained itself are varied and often context-dependent. In some communities, traditional music has survived primarily as a marker of cultural identity and collective memory — performed at weddings, funerals, seasonal festivals, and religious ceremonies in ways that [1] resist the logic of commercial entertainment precisely because their value is inseparable from specific social contexts and lived relationships. The music of the Sámi people of northern Scandinavia, for instance, encompasses the joik — a highly personal form of chant traditionally composed for specific individuals, places, or animals rather than for general audiences — which has persisted not despite its social embeddedness but because of it.", "In other cases, traditional music has survived and even flourished by embracing strategic hybridisation — absorbing influences from dominant global genres while retaining enough of its distinctive melodic, rhythmic, or timbral character to remain recognisably itself. West African music provides some of the most instructive examples of this process. Highlife, Afrobeats, and mbalax all draw deeply on indigenous rhythmic traditions while incorporating electric guitars, synthesisers, and production techniques borrowed from Western popular music. Their commercial success in global markets has, paradoxically, reinvigorated interest in the deeper traditional repertoires from which they emerged, creating a feedback effect whereby international exposure draws younger audiences back toward roots music they might otherwise have bypassed entirely.", "The digital revolution has profoundly altered the landscape in which this negotiation takes place. Streaming platforms and social media have simultaneously posed new threats and opened unexpected opportunities for traditional music. On one hand, algorithmic recommendation systems tend to amplify already-popular content, creating what researchers have described as a \"winner-takes-most\" dynamic in which a small number of globally dominant genres capture a disproportionate share of listening time. On the other hand, the same platforms have enabled traditional musicians in remote communities to reach audiences that would have been entirely inaccessible through pre-digital distribution channels. [2] A Mongolian throat singer, a Carnatic vocalist, or a master of the Vietnamese đàn bầu can now find and cultivate a dedicated global following without the mediation of a major record label, fundamentally altering the economics of cultural survival for niche musical traditions.", "Language represents one of the most significant variables in the resilience of traditional music. Songs that are sung in minority or endangered languages carry with them an additional burden: the potential loss of the linguistic medium itself. Researchers studying the relationship between language endangerment and musical decline have found that the two processes are deeply intertwined — when a language loses its last fluent speakers, the repertoire of songs composed in that language typically becomes inaccessible to new performers, however well the melodies may be preserved in recordings or transcriptions. Conversely, successful language revitalisation efforts — such as those undertaken in Wales, Hawaii, and among several indigenous communities in New Zealand — have often placed traditional song at the centre of their pedagogical strategies, recognising that music offers one of the most affectively powerful pathways into a language's emotional and cultural logic.", "The institutional frameworks through which traditional music is transmitted, taught, and legitimised also matter considerably. In countries where traditional music is integrated into formal school curricula, supported by state cultural institutions, and recognised through professional training pathways, it tends to exhibit greater intergenerational continuity than in countries where transmission depends exclusively on informal apprenticeship or familial inheritance. Ireland provides a notable example: the network of Comhaltas Ceoltóirí Éireann branches, which organise sessions, competitions, and teaching programmes across the country and in diaspora communities worldwide, has been credited with sustaining the vitality of Irish traditional music through periods of rapid urbanisation and emigration that might otherwise have proven fatal to the tradition. [3]", "What emerges from a survey of these varied trajectories is not a single story of either triumph or loss but a more nuanced picture of selective adaptation under conditions of unequal power. Traditional musical forms that have found ways to remain socially meaningful, economically viable, and aesthetically generative — whether through ritual embeddedness, strategic hybridisation, digital distribution, or institutional support — have shown remarkable resilience. Those that have lost the communities that once sustained them, or that have been reduced to folkloric spectacle performed for tourists rather than living practice embedded in daily life, face considerably steeper challenges. [4] The most that can be said with confidence is that the outcome, in any given case, is rarely predetermined — it depends on decisions made by communities, institutions, governments, and individual musicians that are, in principle, still open to influence."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Traditional music has proven more resilient than predicted, surviving and adapting through varied mechanisms, though its future remains uneven and contingent", "Traditional music worldwide is in terminal decline and will largely disappear within the next generation as globalisation intensifies", "Digital technology has single-handedly rescued traditional musical forms that would otherwise have been lost to commercial globalisation", "The survival of traditional music depends almost entirely on government cultural policy and institutional support rather than community-level action"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes the Sámi joik from commercially produced music?", options: ["It is a highly personal form of chant composed for specific individuals, places, or animals rather than general audiences", "It has been officially recognised by UNESCO as an Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity", "It incorporates elements of Western classical music that were introduced during the colonial period", "It is performed exclusively by professional musicians who have undergone years of formal conservatory training"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what paradoxical effect has the global commercial success of genres such as Afrobeats had?", options: ["It has caused younger African audiences to abandon traditional music entirely in favour of internationally recognised styles", "It has led major Western record labels to acquire the rights to traditional African musical repertoires", "It has prompted West African governments to restrict the export of traditional musical forms in order to protect cultural intellectual property", "It has reinvigorated interest in the deeper traditional music from which these genres emerged, drawing younger audiences toward roots music"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do algorithmic recommendation systems on streaming platforms threaten traditional music?", options: ["They actively remove traditional music content that fails to meet minimum listener engagement thresholds", "They prioritise content in widely spoken languages, automatically disadvantaging music sung in minority or regional languages", "They charge traditional musicians higher fees than popular artists for hosting content on their platforms", "They tend to amplify already-popular content, allowing a small number of dominant genres to capture most listening time"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role has traditional song played in language revitalisation efforts in places such as Wales and Hawaii?", options: ["It has served as a source of revenue that funds language documentation and teacher training programmes", "It has been placed at the centre of teaching strategies because music offers a powerful pathway into a language's emotional and cultural logic", "It has provided a written record of vocabulary and grammar that linguists use to reconstruct the structure of endangered languages", "It has been used primarily to attract international interest in endangered languages, raising their global profile"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an activity of Comhaltas Ceoltóirí Éireann?", options: ["Publishing academic research on the history of Irish traditional music", "Organising musical sessions and competitions across Ireland", "Sustaining Irish traditional music through periods of urbanisation and emigration", "Running teaching programmes in diaspora communities worldwide"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"homogenising\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["making different things increasingly similar or uniform", "gradually replacing older forms with newer and more technologically advanced ones", "enriching and diversifying by introducing new influences from outside", "commercialising cultural practices that were previously non-profit in nature"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"preserved\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["restored to its original form after a period of decline or modification", "actively performed and transmitted to new generations of living practitioners", "legally protected under national heritage legislation", "kept in existence in a recorded or documented form"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"Their\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["West African music traditions in general", "Highlife, Afrobeats, and mbalax", "electric guitars, synthesisers, and Western production techniques", "younger audiences drawn back toward roots music"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? \"In this sense, the platform economy has proved to be a double-edged instrument for cultural preservation — capable of both amplifying erasure and enabling survival, often simultaneously.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-084", num: 84, title: "Đề 084", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "How to React When a Dog Attacks", paragraphs: ["Dog attacks are more common than many people realize. According to the World Health Organization, tens of millions of people are bitten by dogs every year around the world, and a significant number of these bites require medical treatment. Children are at particularly high risk, as they are often unable to recognize warning signs from an aggressive dog and may not know how to respond safely. Understanding what to do — and what not to do — when a dog shows signs of aggression can make a critical difference in whether an encounter escalates into a dangerous attack.", "The first and most important rule when faced with a threatening dog is to stay calm. Dogs can sense fear and excitement through changes in body language and scent, and showing panic or running away can trigger a dog's natural chasing instinct . Instead of running, stand very still and avoid making eye contact, as a dog may interpret direct eye contact as a challenge or a threat. Keep your arms close to your body, speak in a low, calm voice if you speak at all, and avoid making any sudden movements. This calm, non-threatening posture sends a signal to the dog that you are not a danger and not prey.", "If the dog continues to approach, place something between yourself and the animal as a barrier. A bag, a bicycle, an umbrella, or even a jacket can be held out to create distance and distract the dog's attention. If the dog lunges and bites, it is better to sacrifice a jacket or bag than to allow the dog access to your body. Do not attempt to pull your arm or hand away if the dog has bitten you, as this action typically causes deeper and more serious wounds. Instead, try to push toward the dog to reduce the biting pressure, which may cause the animal to release its grip. Once free, back away slowly without turning your back to the dog.", "In the event that you are knocked to the ground during an attack, protect yourself by curling into a ball with your knees drawn up to your chest and your hands clasped behind your neck. Keep your face down and your ears covered by your arms. This position protects your vital organs, your face, and your neck — the areas of the body that a dog is most likely to target. Stay in this position and remain as still as possible. Dogs typically lose interest in a person who appears to be inactive, and many victims of attacks have reported that staying still in this position caused the dog to stop and eventually move away.", "After any dog attack, no matter how minor the injury appears, it is essential to seek medical attention as quickly as possible. Dog bites can introduce bacteria deep into tissue and carry the risk of infection, including tetanus and, in some parts of the world, rabies. A doctor will clean the wound thoroughly, assess the risk of infection, and determine whether antibiotics or vaccinations are required. It is also important to report the attack to local animal control authorities, as they can investigate whether the dog has a history of aggression and whether it has been vaccinated against rabies.", "Prevention is, of course, better than any reactive measure. Never approach an unfamiliar dog without first asking its owner for permission. Even a friendly-looking dog may feel threatened if a stranger reaches toward it too quickly or invades its personal space without warning. Teach children to always ask before approaching any dog, to never disturb a dog that is eating, sleeping, or caring for puppies, and to recognize the warning signs of an anxious or aggressive dog, such as a stiff body posture, a low growl, or ears pinned flat against the head. A little knowledge and caution can go a long way toward preventing the pain and trauma of a dog attack entirely."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["The scientific reasons why dogs attack humans and how breeding affects their aggression.", "Practical advice on how to react safely before, during, and after a dog attack, as well as how to prevent one.", "A statistical overview of dog bite incidents worldwide and which dog breeds are most dangerous.", "How to train a dog not to be aggressive toward strangers and unfamiliar animals."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["The attack that must be reported to local authorities.", "The wound that must be cleaned and assessed by a doctor.", "The dog involved in the attack.", "The bacteria introduced into the tissue during the bite."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is recommended when a threatening dog approaches you?", options: ["Back away as quickly as possible while maintaining eye contact with the dog.", "Run quickly in a zigzag pattern to confuse the dog and reach safety.", "Shout loudly and wave your arms to make yourself appear larger and more threatening.", "Stand very still, avoid eye contact, keep your arms close to your body, and speak calmly."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what should you do if a dog bites and holds onto your arm?", options: ["Push toward the dog to reduce the biting pressure, which may cause it to release.", "Pull your arm away as fast as possible to break the dog's grip before the bite deepens.", "Hit the dog firmly on the nose to cause enough pain for it to let go.", "Stay completely still and wait for a bystander to come and help remove the dog."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what position should you adopt if you are knocked to the ground during a dog attack?", options: ["Roll onto your stomach and cover only your face with both hands.", "Lie flat on your back with your arms spread wide to appear as large as possible.", "Curl into a ball with your knees to your chest and your hands clasped behind your neck.", "Sit upright with your legs crossed and your arms raised above your head."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following infections are mentioned as risks associated with dog bites?", options: ["Tetanus and rabies.", "Influenza and E. coli infection.", "Hepatitis and meningitis.", "Lyme disease and salmonella."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an object that can be used as a barrier between yourself and an aggressive dog?", options: ["An umbrella.", "A bicycle.", "A bag.", "A walking stick."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"instinct\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["A temporary emotional reaction caused by fear or excitement.", "A natural, inborn impulse to behave in a certain way without thinking.", "A physical ability that develops as an animal grows older.", "A learned behavior that an animal develops through training and reward."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"victims\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Bystanders who witnessed a dog attack without being harmed.", "Volunteers who help treat injured animals after an attack.", "Experts who study the behavior of aggressive dogs.", "People who have been harmed or injured by something."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's main purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To argue that dog owners should be held legally responsible for all injuries caused by their animals.", "To warn readers that dog attacks are extremely common and almost impossible to avoid.", "To provide practical, step-by-step guidance on how to stay safe during and after a dog attack.", "To compare the effectiveness of different methods of training dogs to prevent aggressive behavior."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Nguyen Nhat Anh and His Influence on Vietnamese Literature", paragraphs: ["Few living writers in Vietnam command the kind of devoted, multigenerational readership that Nguyen Nhat Anh has built over the course of a career spanning more than four decades. Born in 1955 in Quang Nam Province in central Vietnam, he moved to Ho Chi Minh City as a young man and began writing during a period of profound social and cultural change in the country's post-reunification history. His early work appeared in magazines aimed at young readers, establishing a warmth and accessibility of style that would become his most enduring literary signature, and which has allowed him to remain not merely relevant but genuinely beloved across successive generations of Vietnamese readers.", "Nguyen Nhat Anh's most celebrated works belong to a genre that Vietnamese readers call truyện thiếu nhi , or children's and young adult literature, though the label understates the emotional and philosophical depth that his best writing achieves. His novel Cho tôi xin một vé đi tuổi thơ — translated into English as A Ticket to Childhood — published in 2008, is widely regarded as his masterpiece. The book follows a group of children navigating the joys, confusions, and small tragedies of growing up in a provincial Vietnamese town, and it achieves something rare in literature aimed at younger audiences: it speaks to the child's experience with complete sincerity while simultaneously offering the adult reader a meditation on memory, loss, and the irreversibility of time. The novel sold over 350,000 copies in its first year of publication, setting a record for a Vietnamese literary title at that time.", "What distinguishes Nguyen Nhat Anh's writing from that of many of his contemporaries is his treatment of childhood not as a simplified or sentimentalized version of adult life but as a distinct and fully realized world with its own internal logic, moral codes, and emotional intensity. His characters — children and teenagers living in small towns, navigating friendships, first loves, family tensions, and the encroachment of adult responsibilities — are rendered with a specificity and tenderness that readers consistently describe as uncanny in its accuracy. Many Vietnamese adults report that reading his books as children was among the defining experiences of their early lives, and that returning to his work as adults reveals layers of meaning they were unable to perceive the first time.", "Beyond his fiction, Nguyen Nhat Anh has been a significant cultural commentator through his long-running column in the newspaper Áo Trắng and later in other publications, where he wrote under the pen name Bắc Sơn. These columns, which combined humor, nostalgia, and gentle social observation, gave him a public voice that extended well beyond the literary community and established him as one of the most widely read journalists of his generation. His ability to write with equal fluency for children, young adults, and mature readers — shifting register and emotional register with apparent ease — is a versatility that critics have repeatedly identified as the most remarkable aspect of his talent.", "Nguyen Nhat Anh's influence on Vietnamese literature has been both direct and far-reaching. A generation of younger Vietnamese writers has cited him as a primary inspiration, and his insistence on the dignity and seriousness of young adult literature has helped shift the critical conversation about what kinds of writing deserve recognition and institutional support. His books have been translated into several languages, including English, Korean, Japanese, and Thai, extending his readership beyond Vietnam's borders, though the full texture of his prose — which depends heavily on regional idiom, cultural specificity, and the rhythms of spoken Vietnamese — is widely acknowledged to be difficult to translate without significant loss.", "He has received numerous awards for his contributions to Vietnamese culture, including the Ho Chi Minh City Literature and Arts Award on multiple occasions. Despite this recognition, he has maintained a reputation for personal modesty and a deep reluctance to engage in the literary politics and public self-promotion that characterize the careers of many prominent writers. In interviews, he consistently redirects attention away from himself and toward his readers — particularly the children and young people he considers his primary audience — describing the act of writing as a form of conversation with a reader he imagines as very specific, very real, and entirely worthy of his best effort.", "In a literary culture that has sometimes struggled to find a balance between ideological expectation and artistic freedom, Nguyen Nhat Anh's long career represents a quietly remarkable achievement: the building of a body of work that is at once commercially successful, critically respected, and genuinely transformative in the lives of the millions of readers who have grown up with his books beside them."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["An examination of Nguyen Nhat Anh's career, literary style, and lasting influence on Vietnamese readers and literature.", "The challenges of translating Vietnamese literature into foreign languages and reaching international audiences.", "An analysis of why children's literature in Vietnam has historically received less critical recognition than adult fiction.", "A comparison of Nguyen Nhat Anh's writing style with that of other famous Vietnamese authors of his generation."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what record did Cho tôi xin một vé đi tuổi thơ set when it was published in 2008?", options: ["It won the Ho Chi Minh City Literature and Arts Award for three consecutive years.", "It became the first children's novel in Vietnam to be adapted into a major motion picture.", "It sold over 350,000 copies in its first year, setting a record for a Vietnamese literary title at that time.", "It became the first Vietnamese novel to be translated into more than ten foreign languages."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature of Cho tôi xin một vé đi tuổi thơ ?", options: ["It follows a group of children growing up in a provincial Vietnamese town.", "It speaks honestly to the child's experience while also offering adult readers a reflection on memory and time.", "It sold over 350,000 copies in its first year of publication.", "It was based on Nguyen Nhat Anh's own autobiographical experiences growing up in Quang Nam Province."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, under what pen name did Nguyen Nhat Anh write his long-running newspaper column?", options: ["Áo Trắng.", "Bắc Sơn.", "Quang Nam.", "Truyện thiếu nhi."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, into which of the following languages have Nguyen Nhat Anh's books NOT been translated?", options: ["Japanese.", "Thai.", "French.", "Korean."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which award has Nguyen Nhat Anh received on multiple occasions?", options: ["The National Book Award for Children's Literature.", "The Southeast Asian Writers Award.", "The Vietnamese Writers' Association Lifetime Achievement Prize.", "The Ho Chi Minh City Literature and Arts Award."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"enduring\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Lasting over a long period of time without fading.", "Unique to a specific cultural tradition and difficult to export.", "Simple and easy for any reader to understand immediately.", "Controversial and often debated by literary critics."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"His\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["Nguyen Nhat Anh's ability to write with equal fluency for different age groups and registers.", "The pen name Bắc Sơn, which Nguyen Nhat Anh used for his journalism.", "The generation of younger Vietnamese writers who cite Nguyen Nhat Anh as an inspiration.", "The editor of the newspaper Áo Trắng, who gave Nguyen Nhat Anh his first column."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"uncanny\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Technically skilled but lacking emotional warmth or sincerity.", "Remarkably strange or accurate in a way that is hard to explain.", "Old-fashioned in style and unlikely to appeal to modern readers.", "Difficult to understand without detailed knowledge of Vietnamese culture."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the passage about the key reason for Nguyen Nhat Anh's lasting success as a writer?", options: ["His enduring popularity comes from his genuine respect for his readers and his sincere commitment to portraying their experiences truthfully.", "His books succeed mainly because they are short and simple enough for young children to read independently.", "His success is primarily the result of a carefully managed public image and active engagement with the media.", "His reputation has been built largely on the strength of his journalism rather than his fiction."], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Understanding Volcanoes and Their Impact on Human Civilization", paragraphs: ["Volcanoes are among the most geologically significant features on Earth, shaped by forces deep within the planet and capable of reshaping entire landscapes within hours. For scientists and historians alike, they represent a dual phenomenon: engines of creation that have built islands, enriched soils, and altered climates across geological time, and forces of sudden destruction whose eruptions have repeatedly disrupted, displaced, and in some cases annihilated entire human settlements. [1] Understanding how volcanoes form, how scientists monitor them, and how their effects ripple outward through ecology, climate, and human history requires drawing on multiple scientific disciplines simultaneously.", "Volcanoes form where tectonic plates interact in specific ways. At divergent plate boundaries — where plates move apart — magma rises from the mantle to fill the gap, creating volcanic activity along mid-ocean ridges such as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and on land in rift zones such as those found in East Africa. At convergent boundaries — where one plate subducts beneath another — the descending plate melts and produces magma that rises through the overlying crust, forming the explosive stratovolcanoes characteristic of the Pacific Ring of Fire, which encircles the Pacific Ocean and accounts for roughly seventy-five percent of the world's active volcanoes. A third category of volcanic activity occurs at hotspots — stationary plumes of superheated mantle material that burn through the overlying plate regardless of boundary position, creating island chains such as Hawaii and the Galapagos. [2]", "Volcanologists — scientists who specialize in the study of volcanic activity — have developed a sophisticated array of monitoring tools designed to detect the precursors of an eruption before it occurs. Seismographs track the small earthquakes produced by magma movement beneath the surface; tiltmeters and GPS sensors measure ground deformation as swelling magma causes the flanks of a volcano to bulge outward; spectrometers analyze changes in the chemical composition of gases emitted from volcanic vents, particularly increases in sulfur dioxide, which is strongly correlated with rising magma. Satellite-based thermal imaging can detect subtle increases in surface temperature across a volcano's summit and flanks. Together, these instruments have made it possible to issue timely evacuation warnings that have saved tens of thousands of lives in monitored volcanic zones. [3]", "The historical impact of major volcanic eruptions on human civilization has been profound and, in several instances, globally transformative. The eruption of Mount Tambora in Indonesia in 1815 — the largest volcanic eruption in recorded history — injected so much sulfur dioxide into the stratosphere that it reduced global temperatures by an estimated one degree Celsius for the following year. [4] The resulting agricultural failures across North America, Europe, and Asia produced famine conditions so severe that 1816 became known as \"the Year Without a Summer.\" The eruption of Krakatoa in 1883 generated pressure waves that circled the globe multiple times, produced tsunami waves that killed tens of thousands of people along the coastlines of Java and Sumatra, and created atmospheric optical effects — vivid red sunsets caused by suspended volcanic particles — that were observed and recorded as far away as England and the eastern United States.", "Not all volcanic activity, however, is synonymous with destruction. The eruption of Kilauea in Hawaii, one of the world's most continuously active volcanoes, has been adding new land to the Big Island at a measurable rate for decades, demonstrating that volcanic processes are fundamentally constructive on geological timescales. Volcanic soils — particularly those derived from basaltic lava and ash deposits — are exceptionally fertile because they are rich in minerals including potassium, calcium, and phosphorus, which are essential for plant growth. The volcanic islands of Indonesia, the Philippines, and Japan support some of the highest agricultural population densities on Earth, a demographic reality that can only be understood in the context of the extraordinary productivity of volcanic soil.", "Climate science has increasingly recognized the role of volcanism in modulating Earth's long-term temperature record. Large igneous provinces — vast outpourings of lava that occurred over millions of years at several points in Earth's history — have been causally linked to major extinction events, most notably the end-Permian mass extinction approximately 252 million years ago, in which an estimated ninety-six percent of marine species and seventy percent of terrestrial vertebrate species were eliminated. At shorter timescales, the cooling effect of large explosive eruptions has occasionally been proposed as a natural analogue for the engineering concept of stratospheric aerosol injection — the deliberate introduction of reflective particles into the upper atmosphere as a form of solar radiation management. The ethical and practical implications of deliberately replicating volcanic cooling effects at a planetary scale remain deeply contested among climate scientists, engineers, and policymakers.", "What the study of volcanology ultimately reveals is that the planet's interior is not a stable, inert backdrop to human activity but a dynamic system whose processes operate on timescales ranging from seconds to millions of years and whose outputs — in the form of eruptions, gases, soil, and climate forcing — are deeply intertwined with the conditions that make Earth habitable. For communities living in the shadow of active volcanoes, this is not an abstract scientific proposition but a lived reality that shapes every dimension of daily existence, from the crops they grow to the emergency plans they rehearse each year."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To argue that volcanic eruptions pose an unacceptably high risk to human populations and that governments should relocate all communities living near active volcanoes.", "To compare the effectiveness of different monitoring technologies used by volcanologists to predict eruptions.", "To provide a multi-dimensional examination of volcanoes, covering their formation, monitoring, historical impacts on civilization, agricultural benefits, and role in climate change.", "To demonstrate that the benefits of volcanic soil and geothermal energy outweigh the destructive potential of volcanic eruptions."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what percentage of the world's active volcanoes are found along the Pacific Ring of Fire?", options: ["Roughly seventy-five percent.", "Nearly ninety percent.", "Around sixty percent.", "Approximately fifty percent."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does an increase in sulfur dioxide emissions from a volcanic vent indicate to volcanologists?", options: ["That the volcano's crater is filling with rainwater, increasing the risk of a phreatic explosion.", "That tectonic plates in the surrounding region are beginning to diverge more rapidly than usual.", "That a nearby oceanic hotspot has become more active and is likely to form a new island within decades.", "That rising magma is approaching the surface, as sulfur dioxide levels are strongly correlated with magma movement."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence of the 1883 eruption of Krakatoa?", options: ["Tsunami waves that killed tens of thousands of people along the coasts of Java and Sumatra.", "Vivid red sunsets caused by suspended volcanic particles observed as far away as England.", "Pressure waves that circled the globe multiple times.", "A reduction in global temperatures that caused widespread agricultural failures across three continents."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, by how much did the 1815 eruption of Mount Tambora reduce global temperatures?", options: ["By approximately half a degree Celsius for two years.", "By an estimated one degree Celsius for the following year.", "By a fraction of a degree, though the effect was felt most severely in the Northern Hemisphere.", "By nearly two degrees Celsius, causing the coldest decade in recorded history."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what percentage of marine species were eliminated in the end-Permian mass extinction event linked to large igneous provinces?", options: ["Nearly one hundred percent, making it a near-total marine extinction.", "Around eighty percent.", "An estimated ninety-six percent.", "Approximately seventy percent."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"characteristic\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Particularly dangerous and capable of causing widespread destruction.", "Exceptionally rare and found only under very specific geological conditions.", "Newly documented and not yet fully understood by the scientific community.", "Typical or distinctive of a particular place, group, or situation."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"synonymous\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Scientifically proven to be identical in every measurable respect.", "Widely misunderstood by the general public and the media.", "Entirely unrelated and belonging to separate categories.", "Closely associated or regarded as meaning the same thing."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The phrase \"these instruments\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["The chemical compounds released by volcanoes that scientists analyze to predict eruptions.", "The historical records and geological surveys used to reconstruct past volcanic eruptions.", "The seismographs, tiltmeters, GPS sensors, spectrometers, and satellite imaging systems used to monitor volcanic activity.", "The evacuation plans and emergency communication systems deployed by governments in volcanic zones."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Crop failures in North America and Europe drove grain prices to historic highs, triggering food riots in several countries and contributing to a wave of emigration from affected regions.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Importance of Preserving Cultural and Historical Heritage", paragraphs: ["The concept of cultural and historical heritage encompasses an extraordinarily broad range of human artifacts and practices: the built environment of ancient cities, the manuscripts and artworks of vanished civilizations, the oral traditions and ceremonial practices of living communities, the languages spoken by ever-dwindling populations, and the intangible knowledge systems embedded in craft traditions, agricultural practices, and ways of understanding the natural world. What unites these diverse phenomena is [1] the recognition, now encoded in international law through instruments such as the 1972 UNESCO World Heritage Convention, that certain expressions of human creativity and achievement constitute a common inheritance whose loss would diminish not merely the communities from which they originated but humanity as a whole.", "The arguments for heritage preservation are multiple and mutually reinforcing, though they are not all equally compelling to all audiences. The economic case is perhaps the most immediately persuasive in policy contexts: heritage sites generate tourism revenue, support local employment, anchor urban regeneration projects, and contribute to the soft-power projection of national identity on the international stage. The historic centers of cities such as Florence, Kyoto, and Hoi An have become cornerstones of their respective national economies, drawing millions of visitors annually and generating multiplier effects that extend far beyond the boundaries of the sites themselves. At the same time, the reduction of heritage to its economic utility risks a category error — treating as instrumental what is, at its core, an intrinsic good.", "The intellectual and scientific case for preservation is equally substantial. Archaeological sites and historic structures are non-renewable information resources: once destroyed, the evidence they contain about past human behavior, environmental conditions, and cultural practices is permanently lost. The destruction of the ancient library of Alexandria in antiquity, the Taliban's demolition of the Bamiyan Buddhas in Afghanistan in 2001, and the deliberate targeting of the old city of Mosul by the Islamic State in 2014–2015 are all instances in which the erasure of heritage represented not merely aesthetic loss but the obliteration of irreplaceable historical knowledge. Climate change, urban development pressures, and the erosion of minority language communities present slower but no less comprehensive threats to the world's heritage inventory. [2]", "The governance of world heritage raises questions of sovereignty, representation, and power that are considerably more contested than the management of individual sites might suggest. The UNESCO World Heritage List — which currently contains over 1,100 entries — has been criticized for reflecting the institutional priorities and cultural assumptions of its predominantly European founding membership rather than a genuinely global assessment of significance. Sites in Africa, Asia, and the Pacific remain underrepresented relative to those in Europe and North America, a disparity that critics argue perpetuates a hierarchy of heritage value rooted in colonial-era frameworks of what constitutes civilization and achievement. [3] The question of who has the authority to designate a site as globally significant — and by extension, who has the right to determine the conditions under which it may be modified, developed, or interpreted — is deeply entangled with questions of indigenous land rights and cultural self-determination.", "The repatriation of cultural objects acquired during the colonial period has emerged as one of the most contentious fronts in the broader heritage debate. Museums in the United Kingdom, France, Germany, and the United States hold vast collections of artifacts removed from colonized territories under conditions ranging from formal purchase to outright looting, and calls for their return have intensified significantly over the past decade. The British Museum's retention of the Elgin Marbles — a collection of classical Greek sculptures removed from the Parthenon in Athens in the early nineteenth century — has become the most prominent single case in this debate, with the Greek government maintaining a sustained diplomatic campaign for their repatriation. Proponents of retention argue that encyclopedic museums serve a unique educational function that would be undermined by the precedent of systematic repatriation; critics counter that this argument rationalizes the perpetuation of a form of cultural dispossession that would not be tolerated if applied to any other category of property. [4]", "Intangible heritage — the living practices, knowledge systems, and expressive traditions that communities maintain through performance, transmission, and daily use — has historically received less institutional attention than its built and object-based counterparts, despite representing in many cases a more direct and meaningful form of cultural continuity. UNESCO's 2003 Convention for the Safeguarding of the Intangible Cultural Heritage sought to address this imbalance by creating a framework for recognizing and supporting practices ranging from the Mediterranean diet and the art of Neapolitan pizza-making to the oral traditions of the Mongols and the craft of Vietnamese lacquerware. The effectiveness of this framework in actually sustaining endangered practices, as opposed to documenting them for archival purposes, remains a matter of ongoing scholarly debate.", "What the preservation of cultural heritage ultimately demands is a recognition that the past is not simply a resource to be managed or a product to be marketed but a living dimension of present identity whose erosion has consequences that are difficult to quantify precisely because they are so fundamental. The communities most affected by heritage loss — those whose languages, ceremonies, and knowledge systems are disappearing — rarely need to be convinced of this. The more difficult task is persuading those with the institutional power to act that the urgency is real, the timescale is short, and the losses, once incurred, are genuinely irreversible."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the central concern of the passage?", options: ["A proposal for a new international treaty to replace the 1972 UNESCO World Heritage Convention with a more representative framework.", "An argument that economic benefits should be the primary justification used when advocating for heritage preservation funding.", "A multi-layered examination of why cultural and historical heritage matters, the threats it faces, and the contested governance questions surrounding its preservation.", "A defense of UNESCO's World Heritage List as the most effective existing framework for global heritage protection."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which three cities are cited as examples of places where heritage sites have become economic cornerstones?", options: ["Rome, Paris, and Beijing.", "Florence, Kyoto, and Hoi An.", "Athens, Cairo, and Hanoi.", "Istanbul, Dubrovnik, and Luang Prabang."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following instances of heritage destruction is NOT mentioned in the passage?", options: ["The destruction of the ancient library of Alexandria.", "The Taliban's demolition of the Bamiyan Buddhas in Afghanistan in 2001.", "The deliberate targeting of the old city of Mosul by the Islamic State in 2014–2015.", "The burning of Notre-Dame Cathedral in Paris in 2019."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what argument do proponents of retaining the Elgin Marbles in the British Museum make?", options: ["Encyclopedic museums serve a unique educational function that would be undermined by the precedent of systematic repatriation.", "The Greek government has not demonstrated that it has the conservation facilities required to safely house the sculptures.", "The sculptures were purchased legally and in full compliance with the international laws of the time.", "Returning the sculptures would set a precedent that would empty encyclopedic museums of most of their collections."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an example of intangible cultural heritage recognized under UNESCO's 2003 Convention?", options: ["The traditional weaving techniques of the Andean communities of Peru.", "The Mediterranean diet.", "The oral traditions of the Mongols.", "The craft of Vietnamese lacquerware."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"immediately\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Most comprehensively supported by empirical data from multiple sources.", "Most urgently requiring action within a very short time frame.", "Most directly and readily convincing without requiring further elaboration.", "Most recently introduced into the debate by contemporary policymakers."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"obliteration\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Complete and total destruction leaving nothing behind.", "Gradual deterioration caused by neglect and insufficient funding.", "Deliberate reinterpretation that alters the original meaning of an artifact.", "Careful removal and storage in a secure location for future generations."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"contentious\" in paragraph 5 refers to the fact that the repatriation of colonial-era cultural objects is _______.", options: ["A legal process that has already been resolved in favor of the originating countries in most cases.", "A recently emerging issue that has only gained public attention in the past few years.", "A purely academic debate with little practical consequence for the objects or communities involved.", "A matter that generates significant disagreement and dispute between different parties."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view of those with institutional power in relation to heritage preservation?", options: ["The author argues that economic arguments are the only ones likely to motivate institutional actors, and that other justifications for preservation should be abandoned.", "The author believes institutions such as UNESCO and major museums are already doing everything reasonably possible to protect global heritage.", "The author implies a quiet but pointed frustration that institutions with the power to act on heritage preservation have been insufficiently persuaded of the urgency and irreversibility of the losses involved.", "The author suggests that the communities experiencing heritage loss are themselves partly responsible for failing to advocate loudly enough for protection."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Reforming the nomination and evaluation process to give greater weight to the assessments of communities from underrepresented regions has been proposed as one means of addressing this structural bias.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
